{
  "an9.1:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 9.1 ",
  "an9.1:0.2": "1. Awakening ",
  "an9.1:0.3": "Awakening ",
  "an9.1:1.1": "So I have heard. ",
  "an9.1:1.2": "At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. ",
  "an9.1:1.3": "There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: ",
  "an9.1:2.1": "“Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: ",
  "an9.1:2.2": "‘Reverends, what is the vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening?’ How would you answer them?” ",
  "an9.1:2.3": "“Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. … The mendicants will listen and remember it.” ",
  "an9.1:3.1": "“Well then, mendicants, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” ",
  "an9.1:3.2": "“Yes, sir,” they replied. ",
  "an9.1:3.3": "The Buddha said this: ",
  "an9.1:4.1": "“Mendicants, if wanderers of other religions were to ask: ",
  "an9.1:4.2": "‘Reverends, what is the vital condition for the development of the qualities on the same side as awakening?’ You should answer them: ",
  "an9.1:5.1": "‘It’s when a mendicant has good friends, companions, and associates. ",
  "an9.1:5.2": "This is the first vital condition for the development of the awakening factors. ",
  "an9.1:6.1": "Furthermore, a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. ",
  "an